Original commit body from 653caf3c03960390dedf81e527f384c991ac5347:

INV-vavup. ADR-0016's implementation note says command-mediated languages
populate `subprocess` by emitting unresolved external-command edges "not via an
io_primitives data-I/O catalog that would MIS-ATTRIBUTE CURL'S NETWORK ACTIVITY
TO THE SHELL SCRIPT ITSELF."

I read that twice as forbidding ANY bash io_primitives catalogue. It does not.
It forbids exactly the case that would be a mis-attribution and is SILENT on
the case that would be accurate, because a shell has TWO I/O surfaces:

  curl -o /etc/cron.d/pwned     the write is performed by CURL, an opaque
                                external program -> subprocess + opacity;
                                attributing fs_write to the script WOULD be
                                the mis-attribution this note forbids
  echo "$SECRET" > /etc/...     the write is performed by THE SHELL ITSELF
                                (echo is a builtin, and even for an external
                                command the redirection is established by the
                                shell before exec) -> the shell's own
                                primitive, same standing as os.remove in
                                python; cataloguing it is CORRECT

MEASURED CONSEQUENCE OF THE GAP: bash.py dispatches on function_definition /
declaration_command / command only, so on an 8-statement script exercising the
common idioms, the four launches emitted correctly and the THREE REDIRECTION
WRITES TO HOST PATHS PRODUCED ZERO EDGES. `echo $SECRET > /etc/cron.d/pwned` --
the same cron-dropper INV-larol was filed about, written with a builtin and a
redirect instead of `curl -o` -- is invisible.

AND "REDIRECTION IS SYNTAX, NOT A NAMED CALL" IS NOT A BARRIER EITHER, which is
the second thing I asserted and had to retract: this schema ALREADY classifies
non-call constructs through `attributes:` (os.environ, sys.stdout, sys.stdin),
which reach the boundary pipeline via synthesized module_attr_ref edges
(WI-guhok). The precedent is exact and shipped.

The amendment records the two-surface distinction, the measured emission gap,
the attributes precedent, and the SEQUENCING constraint: marking bash
taint-supported on the strength of its launch edges BEFORE redirection writes
are visible would let a redirection-dropper pass the coverage gate and confirm
"never writes to the host filesystem" -- a false confirm through a hole
DISTINCT from the one INV-larol closed. Redirection first, taint-support
second.

Docs-only; ADRs are edited in place per docs/adr/README.md and git history is
the immutable record. No behaviour change. smart-test: no test-relevant files
changed; mypy ratchet clean.

Ref: INV-vavup, INV-larol, WI-guhok, INV-zumin (mode-vs-rows), INV-dabuf.
